<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's the Weekend. – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Weekend.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use pictures or objects to express ideas. Concept: Use facial expressions and simple gestures to help explain feelings or needs.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use simple drawings or pictures to communicate days of the week to peers (e.g., drawing a picture of a calendar showing the days of the week). Can use gestures and facial expressions to express basic ideas about days of the week to others (e.g., pointing to a picture representing a specific day). Can clarify misunderstandings about days of the week using body language (e.g., pointing to the correct day on a calendar).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone! What day is today?" The teacher shows large flashcards of the days of the week (Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday) and points to each day as students chorally repeat the names. The teacher then asks students to show with their fingers how many days are in a week (7). The teacher plays a "Days of the Week" song, encouraging students to sing along and do simple TPR actions for each day (e.g., simulating writing for 'Monday', jumping for 'Saturday').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard for "Saturday" and says, "Today is Saturday." Then, the teacher shows a picture of children playing and smiling, making a relaxing facial expression and saying, "Saturday is exciting! It is the weekend." The teacher then holds up a flashcard for "Monday," makes a "busy" facial expression, and says, "Monday is busy." The teacher explicitly models how facial expressions and gestures can help explain the meaning of words like "exciting," "relaxing," and "busy." The teacher pronounces /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day" clearly, exaggerating the sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of children sleeping peacefully and asks, "Is this picture for 'busy' or 'relaxing'?" (Expected answer: Relaxing!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the word "weekend" on a flashcard and asks, "Is 'weekend' one day or two days?" (Expected answer: Two days!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5–7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, simple picture cards representing activities for each day of the week (e.g., school for Monday-Friday, park for Saturday, home/relaxing for Sunday), alongside flashcards with the written day names (Monday, Tuesday, etc.).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using time-related vocabulary: Identifying words for days and weekends by using or pointing to them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays a large chart titled "My Week!" showing each day of the week with a corresponding simple drawing (e.g., school for Monday-Friday, a happy sun for Saturday, a sleeping child for Sunday). The teacher points to each day and its picture, saying the day and a simple phrase: "Today is Monday. School day." "Today is Saturday. Play day!" "Today is Sunday. Relaxing day." The teacher uses appropriate facial expressions and gestures for each activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to the "Saturday" picture and asks, "What day is this?" Students respond chorally or individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Show me the picture for 'relaxing day'!" Students point to the Sunday picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up the flashcard for "weekend" and asks students to point to the correct days on the chart (Saturday, Sunday).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8–10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Noun + verb + noun ("Today is Monday."), Present simple ("It is the weekend."), WH-questions ("What day is today?"). Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, weekend, day, week, be, today, tomorrow, relaxing, exciting, busy, quiet. Pragmatic: Exchanging questions and answers about the current day using simple phrases like "Today is Monday."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Picture-to-Gesture Match:</b> The teacher displays a set of simple drawing cards (e.g., a school, a book, a playground, a bed, a happy face, a busy face). The teacher says a day (e.g., "Monday is busy!") and students must hold up the picture that best describes it and make a corresponding facial expression/gesture (e.g., 'busy face' and 'school picture'). This reinforces the use of pictures and expressions to mediate meaning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Explain the Day" Relay:</b> Students are in small groups. The teacher whispers a day of the week to the first student in each group. That student must then use only gestures and facial expressions (no words!) to explain the day to the next student, who then repeats the gesture to the next, and so on. The last student says the day aloud. This activity emphasizes non-verbal mediation and clarification of meaning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Days of the Week Charades:</b> Students take turns picking a flashcard with a day of the week (e.g., "Saturday"). They must then use body language, simple gestures (e.g., jumping for Saturday, sleeping for Sunday), and facial expressions (e.g., happy for exciting, tired for busy) to help their peers guess the day. Peers can ask, "Is it Monday?" "Is it the weekend?" The student performing must respond with gestures like nodding or shaking head.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12–15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on the 21st Century Skills Project: "Day of the Week Song." Students will perform their song and use visual aids/gestures to mediate its meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Song Presentation with Visual and Kinesthetic Mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students, in small groups, will present their "Day of the Week Song" (developed in previous lessons) to the class. For each day mentioned in their song (e.g., "Today is Monday, school day!"), they must actively use simple drawings, prepared picture cards, or expressive gestures and facial expressions to clarify and 'explain' the meaning of that day or activity. For instance, when singing "Today is Saturday, exciting day!", they should hold up a picture of children playing, point to a 'weekend' calendar section, and make an excited facial expression and gesture. If a peer seems confused, the presenting group must use more gestures or point to a clearer picture to help clarify the meaning of the specific day or activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher encourages groups to observe each other's presentations and identify which pictures/gestures are most helpful in understanding the song.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10–12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback using simple 'thumbs up'/'thumbs down'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Day of the Week Song" with mediating visuals and gestures. Peers give immediate non-verbal feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day" clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use day names (Monday, Tuesday, etc.) and "weekend" in their song.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation:</b> Students effectively use pictures/drawings or gestures/facial expressions to communicate the meaning of days or activities in their song.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Group members work together to present and mediate the song.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic Use:</b> Students use polite expressions where appropriate during presentation setup or questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the picture you used that helped your friends understand your song the most."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me a happy face if you could explain your song with your body. Show me a thinking face if it was hard."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7–8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of your song was easy to explain with a picture or gesture?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of your song was more difficult to explain without words?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? (e.g., Students effectively used pictures and gestures to clarify song meaning.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., Provide more pre-made visual options for students who struggle with drawing or coming up with gestures, or offer sentence starters for simple verbal mediation.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "It's the Weekend" theme by integrating and mediating the content through the project. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and concepts, building on the communicative skills developed here, potentially requiring students to mediate new information using similar strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3–5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>